  "query_preview": "..\nFriday and Saturday: A deepening trough along the West Coast\nwill track inland Friday into Saturday, sending a dry cold \nfront across the region and bringing a period of widespread \nwindy conditions. Strong winds in combination with hot and dry \nconditions will result in critical fire weather concerns Friday \nand Saturday afternoons. For Friday, winds will be focused\nthrough the Cascade gaps, up the Okanogan Valley, and across \nthe Western Columbia basin with sustained speeds of 10 to 15 mph\nand gusts to 25 mph. Saturday is looking like the more \nconcerning day with stronger, more widespread winds. For \nSaturday, there will be a substantial jump in wind speeds and \ngusts regionwide with the highest wind speeds across the \nColumbia Basin. Sustained speeds across the region will range \nfrom 15 to 25 mph with gusts of 30 to 40 mph. For most areas, \nafternoon humidity values will continue to fall into the teens \nFriday and Saturday. Any new or existing fires will be capable \nof very rapid fire spread. Smoke models depict higher \nconcentrations of smoke moving out of the region over the next \ncouple of days, but that is dependent on the activity of any new\nand ongoing fires. For temperatures, Friday will mark the \nbeginning of a cooling trend with highs running 2 to 4 degrees \ncooler than on Thursday, but it will still be quite warm in the \nmid 90s to just over 100 degrees. Expect more noticeable cooling\non Saturday with temperatures dropping another 7 to 10 degrees,\nputting us back near seasonal normals for late July in the \nupper 80s to low 90s.\n\nSunday through Wednesday: Ensembles generally agree that the \nnext shortwave trough will move onshore on Sunday, bringing \nanother 5 to 7 degrees of cooling, continued breezy winds, and a \nchance for light preciptiation along the Cascade crest. Onshore \nflow into western Washington will continue through the first \nhalf of next week with diurnal cross-Cascade pressure gradients \nbringing breezy afternoon winds across the Inland Northwest. \nHighs will be in the 80s to low 90s, and overnight lows will be \nin the 50s to low 60s. /Fewkes",
